00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.110
But let's make sure that we
make a little bit of time

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.410
to understand why APIs.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.370
I don't spend a ton of time
on this high level stuff,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.420
but I wanted to set
a little context.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.480
Obviously, today's world
of digital business

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.580
is built on APIs.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.530
And APIs are used in a
lot of different contexts.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.820
You know, here we
have an example of how

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.810
one company could offer APIs
that are used by customers,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.919
by partners, by employees, to
host all kinds of back ends

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:11.970
for all kinds of things.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.300
And it's not really a
surprise to us today

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.730
that APIs are being
used in a way that

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.670
enables all kinds of
interesting businesses.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.080
When we often talk
about API with Apigee,

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.720
we almost always
start with this slide

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.820
that we call the
digital value chain.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.760
And it kind of shows all of
the components of the API

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.400
lifecycle, all the way
from on the left where

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.000
we have actual end users,
real people who use web apps,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.430
and native apps,
and various devices

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.740
that consume digital services.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.510
All the way on the
right we have the back

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.540
ends that actually
hosts those APIs.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.820
Those back ends could
be brand new systems.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.460
Maybe you built something on
App Engine, or built it in Node,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.010
and put it on Heroku
or who knows what.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.540
They could also
be legacy systems

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.100
as we saw in the
keynote this morning

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.900
where they were
taking a SOAP system

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.562
and turning it into an API.

00:02:59.562 --> 00:03:01.020
And the stuff in
the middle is what

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.910
really connects everything
together and makes

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.860
the API real.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.960
And there are really
two aspects to it.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.990
And I'm going to talk especially
about the first aspect,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.660
which is connecting
the developer--

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:15.150
the person who builds the
app that uses the API--

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.350
with the API team.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.660
That's you, folks, who
raised your hand earlier,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.120
the people who are
offering the API.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.300
And that's really about how does
a developer learn about the API

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.480
and understand the API and
get access to that API,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.306
and what are some of the best
practices that make an API

00:03:30.306 --> 00:03:31.680
something a
developer will really

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.930
be excited about using,
rather than using it

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.270
because their boss
told them they had to.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.280
At the same time there's
a technical component,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.250
how does the app actually
connects to the API?

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.470
How do we put in the
right layers of security,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.680
and traffic management, caching,
and all the kinds of things

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.900
that we need in order
to take something that

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.540
may be a back end system
that might be brand new,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.300
or might be a legacy system
that wasn't designed to handle

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.610
the volume or the
complexity of the internet,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.650
and of these connected devices?

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.940
And for that we have platforms
like the Apigee API platform.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.430
And again, our demo today
will be focusing mainly

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.110
on the developer
consumption side,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.480
but we'll touch on some
of those aspects as well.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.980
This digital value
chain actually

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.480
can go in either direction.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.630
You can start with sort of a
SOA enterprise software world

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.510
where you start with services
that you want to expose,

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:19.885
and think about exposing them.

00:04:19.885 --> 00:04:22.260
But often it makes a lot more
sense to start on the left.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.410
Think about what you want
to offer to your customers.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.030
And then, by
definition, what you

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.992
want to offer to developers?

00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:30.450
What do you think
they want to use,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.546
and you can build your business.

00:04:32.546 --> 00:04:34.920
And it turns out you may have
seen the slide this morning

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:38.070
and also Ed Anuff did a great
presentation yesterday on this.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.310
There are actually a couple of
different types of APIs, right?

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.220
On the bottom we have
APIs that are services.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.620
These are the kind of APIs
that if we're an enterprise

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.100
computing today, if we ever
had anything to do with SOA,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.710
even if we're talking
about micro services,

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.990
these are the kinds of APIs
that as developers, we're

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.220
pretty comfortable with.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.280
It's an API that offers you a
way to connect to some back end

00:04:59.280 --> 00:04:59.989
service.

00:04:59.989 --> 00:05:01.530
And that's when we
think about things

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.990
like onboarding and governance.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.070
There are also on the left,
however, APIs get used a lot,

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.420
and get used a lot
by Apigee customers,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.400
to enable interactions.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.800
So APIs that enable
interactions are more about

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.570
enabling mobile apps.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.190
And that's where
we get to a case

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.730
where there may be one API
for all the apps in the world,

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.740
or there may actually
be lots of APIs

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.350
that are customized for
different platforms.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.240
This is something that
companies like Netflix

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.270
are known for doing extremely--

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.650
at extremely large scale,
where literally there

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.680
is a different APIs for
every type of device

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.160
because we want that device
to have the best possible user

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.310
experience.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.700
And this is also where some
new technologies like GraphQL

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.880
will come in that let a device
customized their responses it

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.651
needs for the best performance.

00:05:46.651 --> 00:05:48.150
And then, finally,
we have something

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.416
that's starting to be a
new thing for businesses

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.290
that aren't in the
technology business, which

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.390
are APIs as products.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.250
Google's been
offering AP products

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.280
for a long time-- the Maps
API is very old right.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.130
We have-- and all the things
that are part of Google Cloud

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.320
are APIs that are
offered as products.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.660
But now we're seeing
more and more companies

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.122
be able to offer where basically
the API is the product.

00:06:10.122 --> 00:06:11.580
And the way that
they make money is

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.580
by monetizing
consumption of the API.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.922
And these APIs fit
into ecosystems.

00:06:16.922 --> 00:06:18.380
And an important
thing to remember,

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:21.510
I'm going to talk a lot about
the API developer experience

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.370
as if it's coming
from that outer ring,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.170
the public ecosystems.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.210
These are the APIs that
as developers you all

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.560
know and understand.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.990
The Google Cloud APIs,
the Twitter APIs,

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.670
the Facebook APIs.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:36.084
And when people talk about
APIs with Apigee, they often--

00:06:36.084 --> 00:06:37.500
especially a couple
of years ago--

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.999
would start with
the assumption that

00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:41.760
having an API means I
have to offer an API

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.740
to any developer in the world.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.110
But actually, the vast
majority of these APIs

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.510
are more on the internal
part of this ring.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.660
They're used internally,
either within an enterprise,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.710
or they're used by a company
to support applications

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.470
such as mobile applications
and web applications

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.026
that the company builds.

00:07:00.026 --> 00:07:01.650
And we're seeing an
increasing amount--

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.500
and where the API economy
really gets interesting

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.350
is in the middle with these
partnering APIs, in industry

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:08.850
standard APIs.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.860
So where customers are now--

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:14.310
when they used to, for
instance, in financial services

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.370
communicate with trading
partners, and business

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.030
partners, and partners,
and other businesses using

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:26.040
a proprietary format like, Fix,
Swift, or FTPing files around,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.030
or mailing tapes to each other,
increasingly those interactions

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.800
are happening via API.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.550
And that's something we're
going to talk about as well.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.700
So most APIs turns out
are actually internal.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.980
A minority of the
APIs and a minority

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.260
of the API traffic in the
world are these public

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.080
facing external APIs that
have the beautiful developer

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:46.830
portals.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.660
So who cares if
developers love them?

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.430
So let me do a little
bit of evangelism

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.980
so you can at least go back
and tell your friends why it's

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.370
important that we pay attention
to how the API presents itself

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.630
to the developer.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.670
First of all, developers
hate to waste time.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.560
That doesn't mean
developers are lazy.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.170
But it means developers
want to do things quick.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:08.220
So if a developer can pull up
curl, and test out your API,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.170
or if a developer
can call your API

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.660
from a Node.js application
or an Android app

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.420
or an iPhone app with just
a few simple commands,

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:16.770
they are going to be happy.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.010
If the developer's got to
learn a complex proprietary SDK

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.250
and some weird security protocol
and wait for legal approval,

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.610
they're not going to be happy.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.150
And if they have a choice of
APIs, they might move away.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.900
Same thing happens especially
for both internal and external

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.070
developers.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.690
But if you think
about partners, when

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.773
you are offering an API
to your business partners,

00:08:35.773 --> 00:08:38.490
or to your customers as
a business in a business

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.830
to business context, the
thing that we sometimes

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.039
hear and always makes me kind of
frustrated is when people say,

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.111
yeah, it doesn't really
matter what our API looks like

00:08:47.111 --> 00:08:49.110
because our customers
have no choice but to work

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.340
with it because we're huge
company X or huge company Y.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.480
And the truth is that,
yes, you can get away

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:56.730
with a certain amount of that.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:58.980
But unless you can really
make those APIs something

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.080
developers will be
happy to use, you've

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.940
added a layer of friction
to every interaction.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.810
Every sale, every--
everything you do

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.330
becomes that much harder
if the developers are only

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.180
using the API because
they have no other choice.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.970
Successful companies that run
API businesses like Twilio,

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.160
for instance, one of
the reasons they succeed

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.197
is because developers
love to use their APIs.

00:09:19.197 --> 00:09:21.030
And they say why do I
have to go through all

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:24.150
this complicated stuff to use
the thing you made me use?

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.570
Why don't we just use x?

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:31.797
So let's talk about some of
the aspects of great APIs.

00:09:31.797 --> 00:09:33.630
And this is kind of my
list of seven things.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.220
And I'm going to basically
go through each one of them.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.510
But things like self-service
and sign-up and documentation

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.210
are tremendously
important to API adoption.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.550
And if you look at the
APIs that are very popular

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.470
and used widely in
the world, you'll

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.090
see that each one of these
does these in a very good way.

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:50.580
And this is why
we're going to talk

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.320
for a few minutes about
what those things look like.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.520
And then Prithpal is going to
come and show you how to do it.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:57.900
So we'll start
with self-service.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.870
I think self-service is one
of the most important aspects

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.410
of APIs for developers.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.650
Obviously, if you want to offer
an external API to thousands

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.960
and thousands of developers
like Google does,

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.330
you have no choice but
to have self-service.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.430
But increasingly,
as API has become

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.490
what are used for business
to business communications,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.460
and are used inside
a large organization,

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.430
self-service becomes
equally important.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.710
So many companies we
talk to are in a world

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:27.955
where yeah, we have
APIs, but to use them

00:10:27.955 --> 00:10:29.330
you have to know
the guy to call,

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:30.746
and you have to
go to the meeting,

00:10:30.746 --> 00:10:32.330
and you have to send an email.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.130
And then they type
you into a database,

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.706
and it takes three weeks.

00:10:35.706 --> 00:10:37.580
You know, this is slowing
down your business.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:40.790
This is either slowing down
your developers internally,

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.800
or this is slowing
down those partners

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.190
and those customers who could
be happily using those APIs.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.420
So I have some examples here.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:50.290
Walgreens for instance,
these are customers of Apigee

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:51.500
who have produced APIs.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:54.162
They have great self-service
developer portals

00:10:54.162 --> 00:10:56.120
where someone can come
in and they can actually

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.160
see what API is available.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:10:59.210
They can click a button.

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:01.400
And ideally, in a
few minutes they

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.020
can get the security
credentials they

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.904
need to start using that API.

00:11:04.904 --> 00:11:06.320
Now, maybe those
credentials don't

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.760
let them do whatever they want.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.770
Maybe initially
there is a sandbox,

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.170
or there's a low rate limit.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:16.550
But you can get started
quickly and learn the APIs.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:18.890
But very closely
related to self-service

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:20.810
is how you sign up for the API.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.550
So for instance, I
was on this stage

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.950
yesterday with Ismail
from Ticketmaster.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.480
They have a great
developer program.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.080
10% of Ticketmaster's API
traffic and ticket sales

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.390
are now coming from third
party apps, apps that

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.196
were not built by Ticketmaster.

00:11:34.196 --> 00:11:35.570
And one of the
reasons-- and they

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:37.710
achieved this in a
pretty short time.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:39.480
And the reason they
were able to do that--

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.800
in part, of course,
this is my biased view--

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.630
is because their API is
really simple for a developer

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:45.890
to get started with.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:49.490
They have a great portal at
developer.ticketmaster.com.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.349
You sign up.

00:11:50.349 --> 00:11:51.890
And if you look at
the sign-up forum,

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:53.990
there's not a whole
ton of information

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:55.280
you have to give them.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:58.217
On the right, I pasted a
sign on form from Twitter.

00:11:58.217 --> 00:12:00.800
Similarly, yeah, there's a bunch
of legal stuff at the bottom.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.500
And obviously, as a developer,
you need to understand that.

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.870
But they're not asking for
your social security number

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:07.640
or anything like that.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:10.430
In the old days, we used to see
companies who would say, yeah,

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:13.280
to use our APIs you got
to fill out this form

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:14.900
and fax it back
to our legal team.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:16.880
And it takes two
weeks to review.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:18.830
That's death for APIs, right?

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:21.350
You want developers to be
able to at least get started

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:25.250
with those APIs as quickly
as possible via self-service,

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.490
and ideally manage the whole
thing through self-service,

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:32.510
even if approval is needed later
on for higher tiers of APIs

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:36.950
access, or to get access
to production data.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.890
Documentation is also
tremendously important.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:43.610
The rise of formats like open
API, formerly known as Swagger,

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.050
really helps with
this because you

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.404
can specify your API in
a formal way using YAML,

00:12:48.404 --> 00:12:49.820
and there are a
lot of great tools

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.520
out there, including one
in the Apigee Edge platform

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:55.490
that will make it easy for
someone to visualize that API

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.720
and learn that API quickly.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:00.050
So for instance, here we
have Payeezy, you know,

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:01.790
also the Nest API--

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:04.040
you know, these are just
some examples of APIs

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.460
that have really nice,
clear online documentation.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:11.090
And it's not just having good
documentation that's there.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.632
Well, OK, I'm going
to talk about it.

00:13:12.632 --> 00:13:14.589
I thought I was going to
get to the next slide.

00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:15.710
I'll come back to that.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:17.420
But documentation is important.

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:19.700
But automatic
testing is important.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.580
But I'll take a detour as a
sec, and talk about not only

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.910
how the API is documented,
but how it's designed.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:29.270
We can do a whole hour
long session on API design,

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:30.230
and have a lot of fun.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:32.660
And we've done it before
and perhaps we should.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:35.210
The truth is that there are
a couple of different strands

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.520
of thought, and there are
different opinions out there.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:39.532
And people have very
passionate arguments.

00:13:39.532 --> 00:13:41.240
But the thing that
I've noticed is always

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.370
clear is that every
developer I've worked with

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.530
understands what
they don't like.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:48.200
So this slide actually
comes from an old chestnut

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.110
of an Apigee presentation from
Brian Malloy about hey, here's

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:52.644
kind of like, the
bad way to do it.

00:13:52.644 --> 00:13:54.560
And this is how we might
do it if we were just

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.705
programming in a programming
language, or even using SOAP,

00:13:57.705 --> 00:13:58.460
right?

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:00.590
You start out with get
dog and get location,

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.350
but before long, you've got get
recurring dog walk schedule,

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.630
and replace sitting
dogs with running dogs.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.600
And you've made an API
that requires developers--

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.700
they can't make
a single API call

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:13.220
without going through every
line of your documentation

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:16.130
very carefully and
understanding how to do it.

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:19.070
And furthermore, your API
is unlike every other API

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:22.040
in the world, so they have to
go a whole bunch of research

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.220
just to make a single
API call, right?

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.720
At the same time, there are a
lot of API design mechanisms

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:31.880
that developers do understand.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:33.680
If I go in front of
a developer and say,

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:37.264
we have an API that is what is
usually referred to as REST--

00:14:37.264 --> 00:14:38.930
and I'm not going to
spend a lot of time

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.600
on what different people
think the words REST mean--

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:45.320
but if I say that my API
consists of a bunch of URIs

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.050
and a bunch of verbs, and
that to create something

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.690
you post to a URI,
and to get information

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:54.320
about it you do get on that
same API, and to delete it--

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.150
sorry, URI-- and
to delete it you

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.850
do a delete on that
same API, pretty much

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:01.730
every developer will understand
what I'm talking about.

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:04.490
Furthermore, if I
say, OK, then there's

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:06.090
a whole hierarchy of you URIs.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:07.770
And they're documented
in this document,

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:09.950
like the one we saw
a little while back,

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.410
almost every developer
will know how to use it.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.600
And if I say, and by
the way, the thing

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:18.461
that you send back and
fast to my APIs is JSON,

00:15:18.461 --> 00:15:20.960
every developer will know how
to use that and every platform

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.870
in the world, except for
some reason, Java, has JSON

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:24.680
built in.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.020
So they all know
how to work with it.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:29.270
So these are the kinds
of things that work.

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:32.330
On the other hand, there are
some new things coming along

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.690
that are sort of additional
quivers in your--

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.040
arrows in your quiver.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:40.850
For instance, RPC,
represented by Google's gRPC,

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.410
is a very interesting
tool to use

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.890
for APIs that are very
performance critical, where

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.740
you have a pretty good idea of
what the shape of the API is.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:51.090
And it's not going
to change very often.

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.660
Similarly graphQL,
sort of a way that

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:58.190
uses that same you URI and
JSON and verb mechanism,

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:01.940
but it allows a client to
specify exactly what it wants

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:05.180
from the API, potentially
combining many API call

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:06.800
responses into one.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:08.450
And then, finally,
there's Hypermedia,

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:11.150
which, when used carefully,
can make it much easier

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:14.300
for a developer to consume
an API because every API

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:16.190
response has some URLs in it.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:17.600
And all the
developer's got to do

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:21.210
is follow that URL to get more
information about that resource

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.150
and understand what can be done.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.770
And like I said, there's
a ton of stuff out there.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.610
But the basics are if you do
what other people are doing

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:31.380
and follow the patterns
that developers understand,

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:34.300
then there's very
little friction.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:36.510
I did a whole presentation
yesterday on security.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.210
And we're going to keep the
security part real short here.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:41.610
But basically, just
like API design,

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.530
there are security
techniques and standards

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.510
that everyone understands.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:48.270
APIs that need to
authenticate end users,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.940
meaning like, I need
access to my bank account,

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.290
OAuth has become the
de facto standard.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:58.260
OAuth provides a number
of ways that an API client

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.990
can exchange some security
credentials for a token.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:04.740
And that token is now authorized
to do only certain actions

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.020
on behalf of one
and only one user,

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.000
on one and only one device.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.690
Furthermore, it
expires all the time.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:12.774
So you have to keep
refreshing it, right?

00:17:12.774 --> 00:17:14.190
It's become the
de facto standard.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:16.079
And there are a lot
of ways to use it.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:19.109
Similarly, for APIs that
don't require an end

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:22.380
user to be authenticated,
such as store location

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:25.740
APIs, for instance, having
a very simple mechanism

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:29.340
like an API key that allows you
to at least understand which

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.664
application is
making an API call,

00:17:31.664 --> 00:17:34.080
even though the end user doesn't
have to be authenticated,

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:37.500
is also a very important part
of effective API management.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.600
These are things
everyone understands.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.010
Making up your own thing.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.230
You know, hey, I have a
new security mechanism.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.450
I have a proprietary
token format.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.127
Download this
little bit of Java,

00:17:47.127 --> 00:17:49.710
and put it in your client, and
we won't tell you what it does,

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.330
but we promise it's OK.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:55.960
Those things make it harder
for people to adopt your API.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.500
And then, finally, I'll talk
about a few other things.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.150
And now we've done
the basics, right?

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:02.190
We've got a well-designed API.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:04.440
We've got the right
security mechanism.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:06.540
We've got self-service
onboarding.

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:08.547
We've got great documentation.

00:18:08.547 --> 00:18:11.130
You can stop there, and you've
got an API that developers will

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:13.480
find it very easy to work with.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:16.230
But most APIs that are
very widely adopted

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:19.170
these days also come
with a set of STKs.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.060
So for instance, I
pasted a screenshot

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:23.580
from the Google Cloud
APIs where for--

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.780
the Google Cloud has a big SDK
for many of the cloud APIs.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:29.685
And as you can
see, they have SDKs

00:18:29.685 --> 00:18:32.430
in all the major
languages, not only that.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:33.950
Right there, they
link you not only

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:35.940
to basically how
to get the binaries

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:38.460
for your various platforms,
but also here's the link

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:39.970
to the GitHub repo.

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:41.670
So if you have an
issue with the SDK,

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.740
you can understand what it does.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.080
You can compile it yourself
if you don't trust it--

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:47.640
by the way, at Google
we don't trust anything,

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:49.455
so we compile
everything ourselves.

00:18:49.455 --> 00:18:51.120
You can understand the license.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:52.740
And you can submit
pull requests to it

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.406
if it doesn't do
something that you need

00:18:54.406 --> 00:18:56.400
or you don't like the
way it was structured.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.890
Furthermore, if you
designed your API the way

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:03.180
I just talked about, the
SDK is not a requirement.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:04.740
A real anti-pattern
in the world is

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.410
having an API that
only works with the SDK

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:09.300
because the data
format is proprietary.

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:10.620
It's really weird to use.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.290
It uses some weird binary
protocol, like a database,

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:13.882
right?

00:19:13.882 --> 00:19:15.840
You can't use Oracle
without the Oracle client.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.000
And who knows what that
thing is doing, right?

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.890
But if you're working with an
open API that's well-designed,

00:19:20.890 --> 00:19:23.640
a developer doesn't
have to use the SDK.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.790
But if you provide them with
SDKs for various platforms,

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:29.699
you make them open source, is
a lot easier for developers

00:19:29.699 --> 00:19:31.740
to feel good about your
code if it's open source,

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:33.281
especially if they're
going to put it

00:19:33.281 --> 00:19:35.210
in their proprietary native app.

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:37.085
Now you've made things
easier for developers.

00:19:37.085 --> 00:19:38.460
You've have made them happy.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:41.180
And they're more productive.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.790
And sort of a little bit more
you know state of the art

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:48.090
is more and more APIs now are
able to offer live testing.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:50.150
They're actually able
to not only combine

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.970
that API documentation,
but with the ability

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:56.600
from within your web browser
to actually test API calls

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:58.220
and learn how they work.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:19:59.870
You can try out
different parameters.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:01.952
You can see with
the JSON looks like.

00:20:01.952 --> 00:20:03.410
You can figure out
how to parse it.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:06.620
The more complex an
API gets, the more cool

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:09.020
it is to be able to
actually test that API.

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:11.089
So for instance, Pay
Easy on the right

00:20:11.089 --> 00:20:12.380
has a whole mechanism for that.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:13.630
I was looking at Ticketmaster.

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.320
They have it as well, right?

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:16.520
Really simple APIs.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:17.490
This curl is fine.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.520
But it's really nice for a
developer to say, oh, here

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.050
are the docs for your API.

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.500
You can have a button right
there that says, try it.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:28.270
And now you've made life a lot
easier for your developers.

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:29.910
So with that said,
I'm now going to go

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.812
on to how you do this
yourself, and Prithpal

00:20:31.812 --> 00:20:34.020
from the Apigee team is
going to come up and give you

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:35.480
guys a demo.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:36.905
PRITHPAL BHOGILL: Thanks, Greg.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:44.040
Can we switch the demo, please?

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:44.650
Awesome.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:45.150
All right.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:48.240
So somehow I'm
confined to this desk

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.610
today so that I can
talk on the microphone

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:51.662
and you guys can hear me.

00:20:51.662 --> 00:20:53.370
But I'll try my best
to kind of hand-wave

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:56.454
because I usually like to
move around quite a bit.

00:20:56.454 --> 00:20:58.620
Just a quick show of hands
out here, how many of you

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:02.040
folks in attendance have
actually worked with, or used,

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:05.871
or seen a developer
portal before?

00:21:05.871 --> 00:21:06.370
OK.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.060
Quite a few.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.200
Thanks, Greg.

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:14.530
So as Greg touched on some
very important things upfront,

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:19.000
the adoption of our APIs is one
of the most important things,

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:19.600
right?

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:23.710
Who cares if we have good APIs
and, if they're not adopted,

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:26.570
you really don't make
too much out of it.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:29.360
So in this session,
in this segment,

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:34.210
I'm going to focus on what
really makes for an awesome API

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.630
developer experience,
primarily for the app developer

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:41.110
audience who is tasked
with building apps, right?

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:45.730
So in this case, I'm going to
leverage the backdrop of Fazio,

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:47.935
who's an online shoe retailer.

00:21:47.935 --> 00:21:50.740
I'll use this as an example.

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:57.460
And in my role, pretend
I'm a partner developer.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:02.570
So Fazio happens to be
selling shoes online.

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:06.410
And they have a very
broad channel ecosystem.

00:22:06.410 --> 00:22:08.320
So you have a lot of
different partners

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:11.290
who can essentially
leverage the Fazio APIs,

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.360
and build their own apps
to sell Fazio products.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:15.040
OK?

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:20.180
So just as in your
own experience today,

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:25.030
if you're using a mobile app,
and if you spent more than four

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:29.110
to six seconds on the very
first page of a mobile app,

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:30.960
chances are you're
going to the App Store

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:32.740
to look for another app.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:36.130
Something very similar is
happening in the API arena.

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.170
As Greg mentioned, if
they have a choice--

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:39.580
and many of them do--

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:41.650
an app developer is going
to go to another API

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:45.250
very quickly if they can
understand what the API does.

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:48.550
So let's examine the journey
of me, the partner developer,

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:50.770
trying to see how
I can build an app.

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.950
So I come to the Fazio
developer portal here.

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:58.480
I can see some simple graphics
around what Fazio does.

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:00.820
More importantly, I can
see three high level

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:03.430
classifications--
product APIs location

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:06.340
APIs, fulfillment APIs.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:08.230
You can see that
I'm not signed in.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:10.640
So my experience is a
little bit different.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.550
I do have access to
some things known

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:14.890
as forum, which will
give me some more

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:19.270
insight into what people are
generally doing with the APIs.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:23.920
So think about the experience
of me trying to register.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:27.250
And I want access to some
of the API documentation

00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:30.160
so I can build an app, and
incorporate some of the order

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:33.530
API within my ecosystem.

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:36.070
So it's a very simple
registration process.

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:38.710
I can just go,
enter my first name,

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:42.730
give it a user name, email,
answer a basic math question,

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:44.480
and then create an account.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.620
In this case, I actually
already have an account.

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:50.900
So I'm going to
sign in out here.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:54.260
And they happen to have
different kinds of rules

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.800
which are supported out here.

00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:00.130
So assuming the demo
gods are with us,

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:04.320
and that I can answer the
basic math question correctly.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.450
They should let me in.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:07.350
Great.

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:10.320
So I just logged
in as a developer.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:11.640
I created an account.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:12.750
I logged in.

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.120
And you can see my
choices open up.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:18.700
I have access to
the API catalog.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:20.640
One of the simplest
things that I

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:23.700
want to do as an
app developer is

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:27.030
for me to be able to browse
the API documentation

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:28.980
and get to the orders API.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:32.350
When I come into the
API catalog section,

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:34.605
you can see that I can see
a bunch of different APIs

00:24:34.605 --> 00:24:37.790
out here, around
customer authentication.

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:43.584
Let's just unselect all of
them, and just click on orders.

00:24:43.584 --> 00:24:45.500
One of the things that
Greg had also mentioned

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:47.390
was API as products.

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:50.060
This is another example
of how Fazio is now

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:53.720
trying to offer their
APIs as products

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:56.600
which can be consumed
by their channels,

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:58.620
by the partner ecosystem.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:03.020
So let's take a look at
the Fazio orders gold tier.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:05.900
And this just uses a
different mechanism

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:08.480
where you can subscribe to
more number of API calls

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:12.000
if you were to pick a silver
option or a regular option.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.470
So great.

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:16.080
The next what I do is I
want to try and create

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:22.260
an app by which I can place
an order on the Fazio API.

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.932
So then I come to
the orders API page.

00:25:24.932 --> 00:25:26.640
You can see that
they've done a fantastic

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:30.980
job of clearly documenting
how to create an order,

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:34.580
get an order, cancel
order, so on and so forth.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:37.650
Becomes very simple for me
to imagine how to use this.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.250
So let's go click
on the Create Order.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.820
And again, this is
not a developer portal

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.780
where you have a link
to an API documentation

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:48.510
which you can go, download,
and use some testing tool.

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:50.840
This all first in
line testing for me.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:55.140
So as the app developer, I have
signed into the Fazio developer

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:58.500
portal, and I'll do a
live API testing here

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:01.840
without presenting
any other tool.

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:03.870
So in the create
order, I can see

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:06.240
that I have a good
amount of help

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:10.170
if I need to to explain what
each and every field does.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.210
More importantly,
they're already

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:15.780
put in a very
simple test payload.

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:16.560
OK.

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:18.960
You can see that this
API is protected.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.970
I already set the
OAuth credentials.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:24.120
If not, you can click
on this button, sign in,

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:27.030
and it will already attach
an OAuth token for you.

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:27.540
OK.

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:31.880
So it's a very simple
submit order request.

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:38.730
And if I go down here, and
click on a send desk request,

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:43.270
it's sent this order
in, and I got a 200 OK.

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:44.160
Right?

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.820
A couple of clicks.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:48.250
I went into the API
catalog, was able to look

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:50.170
at the create order API.

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:51.720
I know the payload.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:52.390
I click that.

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:53.680
I'm good.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:57.160
So that's one aspect of
my app experience, right?

00:26:57.160 --> 00:27:00.310
It looks like a
pretty simple API.

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:01.660
So great.

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:03.850
How do I get started?

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:06.370
Greg also mentioned
about how do you really

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:08.020
eliminate the friction?

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:11.580
Do you really want to be able
to have this developer send

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:14.680
in a request so they can get
their own API keys to access

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:17.440
the API, or a fax
machine and wait to get

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:19.300
an approval to weeks back?

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:22.810
That's certainly going to
just throw the developer away.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:26.260
Looks like the developer portal
provides a much more simpler

00:27:26.260 --> 00:27:27.820
mechanism to do that.

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.610
So they have the My
Apps section here.

00:27:30.610 --> 00:27:32.320
I'm going to click on this.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:33.940
And as you can
see, this developer

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:36.520
has already done some
stuff in the past

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:42.420
I'm going to just say I want
to register a brand new app.

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:47.750
So let's call this thing
My First Shoe Ordering App.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:28:00.180
And I'm going to pick
this orders product.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.980
An API product is
nothing but a mechanism.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:04.670
It's a grouping of
certain APIs which

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:08.400
are available at a
specific price point,

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:11.310
or at a specific volume metric.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:15.240
So let's go and do this,
and click Create app.

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:18.120
So using this as
an app developer,

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:24.010
I've just asked for
access to the orders API.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:29.520
And you can see that out here,
I can see the status as pending,

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:30.330
right?

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:34.720
So as a developer, I'm just
waiting to get access to this.

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:36.300
I've initiated a request.

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:39.450
Now let's hop over
to the provider side.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:41.910
This is the approval's
side of the house.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:43.740
This is the product
manager who'd

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:47.580
be sitting on the side trying to
approve any app requests which

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:48.365
come in.

00:28:48.365 --> 00:28:51.120
So let's take a
quick peek at that.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:54.660
So in this case, I'm logged
into the Apigee Edge user

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:58.020
interface, which is a portal
that the providers are

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.890
going to be using.

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:03.410
So in this case, I go
on the publish icon,

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:09.640
click on the apps
link, and let's

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:12.260
just type in the word shoe.

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:14.870
Fair enough, I can see
the My First Shoe Ordering

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:18.110
App, which has been
requested to be created,

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:22.430
and given permission to
by this new app developer.

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:24.980
Within the app
details section, you

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:27.945
can see sure that it's
in a pending status.

00:29:27.945 --> 00:29:29.680
So I'm going to click on Edit.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:36.420
And I'm going to approve
this app right here.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:41.070
Let's go ahead and save this.

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:47.520
Just in couple of clicks I've
been able to approve the app.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:51.180
And if I'm an app
developer, I can now

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:54.170
see that this app is approved.

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:56.220
So what happened
when we did that?

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.530
Well, let's take a look.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:01.020
As part of the app
approval process,

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:04.590
I actually now have my own
set of consumer key in secret

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:08.190
that I can actually use
to securely call this API.

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:09.570
This is very important.

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:13.080
There was no human involved,
but from a provider perspective,

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:15.030
you can automate that
kind of approval.

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:17.040
There could be
certain other APIs

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:21.030
they do make provision for which
it gets auto approved, right?

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:22.710
So that's very typical
of many customers

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:25.230
who may do that for
some low risk APIs,

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:28.250
or where they do not need
any kind of oversight.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:31.340
And then, I as the developer
can go and take a look

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:33.620
at different pieces of the apps.

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:38.300
Then I start to work with
some of the app calls

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:39.660
that I make over time.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:41.660
It would also surface
some of the analytics that

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:43.600
are useful for me
as an app developer,

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:45.830
not from the
enterprise, but to get

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:49.780
a sense of how much did
this app kind of bring up.

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:50.280
OK.

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:52.890
So let's do something
real quick out here.

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:55.230
I'm going to show the
providers out of the house,

00:30:55.230 --> 00:31:02.330
and talk about one simple
aspect of how does this app--

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:07.850
how does the API
provider essentially

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:11.970
ensure that this app
is not abusing the API.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:12.650
OK.

00:31:12.650 --> 00:31:16.080
So in this case, I could
go on the Create Order,

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:18.660
and pretend this app
for some reason was not

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.850
designed with the right intent.

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:25.080
And you can see that I can
continue to just pound on it

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:26.420
a few times.

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:30.020
And I can still submit
a request, right?

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:33.440
On the API provider's side,
there's some very quick checks

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:35.570
and balances that you can take.

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:37.910
In this case, I
need to make sure

00:31:37.910 --> 00:31:41.280
that this API is protected.

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:45.560
So let's take a look at
this underlying API proxy.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:49.180
An API proxy is
nothing but a facade.

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:50.830
That is the thing
which actually sits

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:54.280
in between the consumer
of the app or the API

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:56.630
and the actual API itself.

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:58.414
And that's where all
the magic happens.

00:31:58.414 --> 00:31:59.830
You have a lot of
different policy

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.800
that you can draw up in out
there to enable production.

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:07.000
So in this case, I'm going
to do something very simple.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:10.260
I'm going to attach a
spike arrest policy.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:13.420
A spike arrest policy
something very simple.

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:16.850
All it does is once
you create that--

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:22.010
you can see me adding
a live policy out here.

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:26.920
And this policy I can
configure the rate at which I

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:31.540
want to limit all the API calls
coming into the API proxy.

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:34.440
And the idea is I want
to protect my back end

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:38.560
API from crashing and
burning if any app goes

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:42.310
rogue for some odd reason.

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:45.780
So I've added a very
simple spike arrest policy,

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:49.170
which in this case happens
to be three requests

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:50.530
to that API per minute.

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:56.580
So let's see the effect
of what happens out here.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:33:00.870
So if I'm within my limits,
is going to work just fine.

00:33:00.870 --> 00:33:03.450
But if the app starts to
grow a little bit more crazy,

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:06.972
you can see that it truly
is spike arrest violation

00:33:06.972 --> 00:33:07.680
right about here.

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:14.190
So hopefully, that
gave you a good taste

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:17.070
of what the app
developer experience is.

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:20.090
We looked at how things
can be provisioned in a way

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:22.920
so you cut all the friction
with app developers.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:24.720
They can adopt your APIs.

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:28.080
You also have some tools
on the provider's side

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:31.080
to be able to protect their
APIs from intentional or

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:33.270
unintentional kind of attempts.

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:35.160
And I would like
to now welcome--

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:38.370
if you can go back to
the slides, please--

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.190
I would like to
welcome on the stage

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:42.510
Adam Brancato from Citrix.

00:33:47.904 --> 00:33:49.570
ADAM BRANCATO: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:51.600
My name is Adam Brancato.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:55.680
I am the manager of customer
applications at Citrix.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:58.770
So basically what that
means is my department

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:02.400
is in charge of all the
applications and services

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:06.420
that deal with customer
accounts, contact information,

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:09.300
single sign on, et cetera.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:11.960
So real quick let
me explain to you

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:15.690
guys who Citrix is for
anyone who doesn't know.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:19.230
Our products and services, they
focus on unifying apps and data

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:21.449
in secure digital
work spaces that

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:27.719
enable users to work remotely
from anywhere on any device.

00:34:27.719 --> 00:34:30.810
We have a couple products
that support this.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:33.719
We have our XenApp and
XenDesktop products,

00:34:33.719 --> 00:34:36.870
which are application and
desktop virtualization

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:38.020
products.

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:40.060
We also have
ShareFile, for example,

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:42.960
which is our enterprise
file storage,

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:44.699
file sharing, et cetera.

00:34:44.699 --> 00:34:47.340
Actually we just
recently released

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:50.699
a beta that actually
integrates with G Suite.

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:54.060
Then we have a couple appliance
products such as NetScaler.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:59.130
It's load balancing, firewall
products, et cetera, as well as

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:02.190
internet of things products.

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:03.810
So I'm going to
talk to you today

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:06.360
about the Citrix
API journey, where

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:10.440
we began, where we are now,
how we got started with Apigee.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:14.340
So as Greg talked
about, a lot of times

00:35:14.340 --> 00:35:17.880
you have these
internal services that

00:35:17.880 --> 00:35:19.530
are not very easy to expose.

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:22.740
Or whenever people want
to use the services,

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:24.450
they have to jump
through a lot of hoops.

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:26.730
And that's kind of how
it started at Citrix.

00:35:26.730 --> 00:35:29.102
So we had several
other departments.

00:35:29.102 --> 00:35:30.810
There's a lot of
different IT departments

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:32.130
and IT groups in our company.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:33.900
We have like, 9,000 employees.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:37.110
So I'm sure anyone in
medium to large company

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.690
knows what it's like
with multiple IT groups.

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:43.950
Well, our group is the keeper
of the data for accounts,

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:47.490
contact, single sign on, all
those items that I mentioned

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:48.610
earlier.

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:50.280
So a lot of them
wanted access to this.

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:52.430
Well, a lot of our
systems are written

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:54.460
in legacy SOAP services.

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:59.300
For example, we have
live WCF .NET services.

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:02.030
And so many new
applications are front end,

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:04.460
you know, JavaScript,
Angular, et cetera,

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:07.370
that really don't
play well with SOAP.

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:09.590
Or the way the
system was set up,

00:36:09.590 --> 00:36:11.750
it's not very easy
to give you access.

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:14.630
If you're like, hey, can I have
access to this account service?

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:18.140
I'm like, sure, give me three
days reach out to network guys

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:19.820
to get your account
provisioning.

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:22.670
And then oh, by the way,
here's this really crazy WSDL

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:27.110
that you have to use if you
want to just get Bob's account

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:29.290
information from
his email address.

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:32.630
So at Citrix were looking
for a way, both internally

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:37.700
and externally, to allow
developers, partners, vendors

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:39.860
to access this
information without having

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:43.520
to jump through all these hoops
because it provides a bad API--

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:46.380
or I'm sorry, a bad service
experience like Greg

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:47.840
talked about.

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:50.300
So we started-- we looked at--

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:53.300
we researched several
different APIs platforms.

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:55.490
And we decided on Apigee.

00:36:55.490 --> 00:36:58.847
We created a simple POC in
order to check out the features,

00:36:58.847 --> 00:37:01.430
make sure these guys knew what
they were really talking about,

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:02.805
and if it would
be something that

00:37:02.805 --> 00:37:04.370
would be a good fit for Citrix.

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:06.980
And we decide to move
forward with that.

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:10.400
Now, in production,
we have over 20 APIs.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:12.560
You can see them listed up here.

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:17.090
We have single sign on account
APIs, contacts, support cases.

00:37:17.090 --> 00:37:19.910
There is actually a
Salesforce API you see listed.

00:37:19.910 --> 00:37:21.162
That's interesting.

00:37:21.162 --> 00:37:23.120
Behind the scenes, that's
not even our service.

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:26.420
So we use Salesforce
for our CRM system.

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:29.150
And they have SOAP
services that allow

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:30.920
us to access our customer data.

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:35.960
So we put API management
Apigee on top of that in order

00:37:35.960 --> 00:37:38.390
expose that data out.

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:40.100
And like I said, all
of our APIs are used

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:42.930
use both internal and external.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.840
And it's a simple platform.

00:37:44.840 --> 00:37:48.080
So the developers consuming
them, internal and external,

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:48.710
they love it.

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:49.960
We give them a key.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:50.770
We sign them up.

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:52.100
It's real quick and simple.

00:37:52.100 --> 00:37:53.516
And they're off
the ground running

00:37:53.516 --> 00:37:55.670
in a very, very
short amount of time.

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:58.040
And then our internal
developers who are actually

00:37:58.040 --> 00:38:01.160
configuring Apigee and
these services, you know,

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:02.730
it's real simple to use.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:04.010
It's easy to issue keys.

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:07.900
It's easy to do a lot
of different things.

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:09.890
So I'm going to talk about--

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:13.460
Citrix is one of our first
big use cases that we had.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:17.970
So to give you a little
bit of background,

00:38:17.970 --> 00:38:19.980
we have an e-commerce
platform that we're

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:23.100
using to sell product
to small businesses

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:24.780
and also issue trials.

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:28.470
One of the problems with this
was that for the license keys,

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.710
they had to be uploaded
manually by the business.

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:34.200
And any time we'd run
out of stock on one,

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:36.720
someone would get an email
saying, hey, you know,

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:39.300
XenDesktop, we're
out of product keys.

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:42.030
You know, and someone would
have to go and upload new ones.

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:43.740
And those were
generated internally.

00:38:43.740 --> 00:38:45.840
And it was not a simple process.

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:48.250
So the business came
to us and said, hey,

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:50.820
is there a better way
that we can do this?

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:53.490
Because this was
almost one person's

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:56.340
full time job to manage
these product keys

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:58.170
and upload them because
we had something

00:38:58.170 --> 00:39:00.780
like 50 different products in
the store, 50 different skews

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:01.280
at least.

00:39:06.290 --> 00:39:08.960
So that was our first
use case, right?

00:39:08.960 --> 00:39:11.540
And this vendor
said, oh, we have

00:39:11.540 --> 00:39:15.350
a system where we can make calls
after the order is complete.

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:16.200
We're like, great.

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:17.640
That's terrific.

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:20.090
However, here is
this crazy schema

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:24.770
that we're going to send you,
and you have to use this schema

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:26.210
and accept it.

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:29.690
And that's how it is,
just like Greg was saying.

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:30.710
They're big enough.

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:32.870
They're like hey,
here's our contract.

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:34.730
You have to abide by it.

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:36.420
So we didn't want to do that.

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:40.820
And Apigee was the perfect
solution to this problem.

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:43.130
So if you look up here
on this next slide,

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:45.580
you can see how our solution
was actually set up.

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:49.420
So at the bottom you could see
the external e-commerce site.

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:53.020
They send us custom
ridiculous schema.

00:39:53.020 --> 00:39:54.687
We transform that.

00:39:54.687 --> 00:39:56.020
And then we send it over to you.

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:59.380
You could see the Citrix license
WCF service that's a legacy

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:02.050
.NET service that's been around
at the company for quite some

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:03.070
time.

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:05.980
So to expose that,
all we had to do

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:09.250
was configure Apigee, create
an XSLT that I created.

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:10.660
And it takes only
the values that

00:40:10.660 --> 00:40:11.890
are needed for the service.

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:13.420
It sends it the service.

00:40:13.420 --> 00:40:15.690
The service brings
back the SOAP response,

00:40:15.690 --> 00:40:20.020
converts it back into the
vendor schema, and sends it off.

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:24.510
Then once we had that up
and running, our trials team

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.820
heard about this and
said, wow, they said,

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.170
you guys have an API now?

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:29.610
We'd love to use that.

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:31.890
However, the trials team,
as you'll see in a bit

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:33.870
when I show you
guys a demo, they

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:35.730
have just a simple
front end site,

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:38.190
and they have some front
JavaScript developers,

00:40:38.190 --> 00:40:40.277
and they need that
information in REST.

00:40:40.277 --> 00:40:41.360
And we said, that's great.

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:42.690
Here you guys go.

00:40:42.690 --> 00:40:45.710
Now there's a REST front end on
it, as well as returning JSON.

00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:46.800
Does the same thing.

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:48.860
It hits the same
service on the back end.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:51.090
But on the front end, you know,
it's really easy for these guys

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:51.590
to use.

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:54.040
They send us the
information in JSON.

00:40:54.040 --> 00:40:56.160
We create the license
key, and send it back.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:59.280
And then, finally, we
have SOAP consumers

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:02.730
who just send us the actual
schema that our service uses.

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:06.220
And we sent it back their
keys in that format.

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:08.730
So we have one back
end service that's

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:11.890
exposed over three
different protocols.

00:41:11.890 --> 00:41:15.330
When we deployed all
these different solutions,

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:17.550
there was no back end
service change needed.

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:20.550
All of this was done
through configuration

00:41:20.550 --> 00:41:21.990
on the Apigee side.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:23.670
Very simple to use.

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:25.980
Developers were like,
here's your key.

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:26.880
There you go.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:28.660
And they're off and running.

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:30.807
So just to show you real quick--

00:41:30.807 --> 00:41:32.640
I don't even know if
you guys can read this,

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:35.850
but on the left hand side you
could see that vendor schema.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:37.590
And that's not pretty.

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:39.160
That's what our vendor said.

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:39.930
Here you go.

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:42.032
You go write a
service that takes it.

00:41:42.032 --> 00:41:43.740
We didn't want to have
to deal with that.

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:46.000
And you can see that there's
a scroll bar up there.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:47.940
So that's not even
their entire schema.

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:50.670
They send every single
piece of information

00:41:50.670 --> 00:41:52.780
they have about the order.

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:56.130
In the right hand side ,
you see our Citrix schema,

00:41:56.130 --> 00:41:58.500
which is only about eight
to 10 different inputs,

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:00.300
a couple of different skews.

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:01.470
And they're off and running.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:02.760
And that's it.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:07.670
So Apigee really helped us
move from this crazy schema

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:10.590
to our simple schema,
send it to the back end.

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:12.420
And then, also,
on the trial site,

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:13.950
those guys just send us JSON.

00:42:13.950 --> 00:42:16.370
And we convert it to
the schema you see here.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:20.340
And it gives them the
result they're looking for.

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:24.110
So real quick, I'm going
to show you guys something.

00:42:24.110 --> 00:42:24.971
We switch.

00:42:24.971 --> 00:42:25.470
There we go.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.280
So as much as I'm
sure Prithpal would

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:31.780
love if I messed
with his account,

00:42:31.780 --> 00:42:34.850
I'm going to log out of
his account real quick.

00:42:34.850 --> 00:42:35.590
Yeah, no problem.

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:44.375
And then I'm going to
sign in to my account.

00:42:55.961 --> 00:42:56.460
Cool.

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:59.610
So looks like it
did not like that.

00:43:08.741 --> 00:43:09.240
OK.

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:12.300
So real quick, I'm going
to open up our proxy.

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:27.420
And I'm going to go to
the trace tab real quick.

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:31.340
So this was actually shown
this morning for the keynote,

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:33.750
for anyone that was there.

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:35.910
This is actually the
Apigee trace screen.

00:43:35.910 --> 00:43:37.710
So what this allows
you to do is this

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:42.030
allows you to run a Live Trace
and watch an API call as it

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:45.180
comes through, inspect
the data, and see

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:47.310
what's going on, if there's
any kind of problems.

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:50.700
As developers, we really like
that because if one of our end

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.970
users reports a problem,
this makes it very easy

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:56.760
to determine if it's something
in the Apigee configuration,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:59.020
or if it's something in
our back end service.

00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:00.450
We can inspect the data--

00:44:00.450 --> 00:44:01.900
see what's going on.

00:44:01.900 --> 00:44:04.260
So I'm going to start that
real quick in this tab.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:12.342
And then over here, I'm
going to go to Citrix.com.

00:44:12.342 --> 00:44:14.532
Now I'm going to
go to downloads.

00:44:14.532 --> 00:44:15.990
And I'm going to
click free trials.

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:24.670
So I'm going to choose the--

00:44:24.670 --> 00:44:25.718
let's go with XenApp.

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:30.960
And then I'll
click Download Now.

00:44:35.550 --> 00:44:38.280
So we'll start out--
there's actually four APIs

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.260
that our trial
department is using here,

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:41.754
all in this one page.

00:44:41.754 --> 00:44:43.170
So the first thing
we have here is

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:44.790
says enter your email address.

00:44:44.790 --> 00:44:47.280
And we will send
your trial license.

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:49.230
So I enter my email address.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:51.810
And this is where the
first API call takes place.

00:44:51.810 --> 00:44:53.510
What it's doing
is it's checking.

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:55.540
It's calling API
and saying, hey,

00:44:55.540 --> 00:44:58.560
does Adam Brancato have
an account with us?

00:44:58.560 --> 00:44:59.370
And it did.

00:44:59.370 --> 00:45:00.806
It found my account.

00:45:00.806 --> 00:45:03.180
If it didn't find my account,
what it would have done is,

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:05.410
instead, it would have
displayed a different screen,

00:45:05.410 --> 00:45:10.476
which has very compact number of
fields to create a new account.

00:45:10.476 --> 00:45:11.850
The trials
department, they said,

00:45:11.850 --> 00:45:14.183
we don't want to direct
somebody all the way to the site

00:45:14.183 --> 00:45:16.260
in order to go through
the full account screen

00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:18.446
because that's potentially
going to deter them

00:45:18.446 --> 00:45:20.320
from coming back and
downloading the product.

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:23.340
So instead, they created
a compact screen here

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:26.080
if they don't have an account.

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:32.700
So I'm going to
log in and pretend

00:45:32.700 --> 00:45:35.612
that I've read and accepted
the end user license agreement.

00:45:38.920 --> 00:45:40.090
And hit Send License.

00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:44.480
So now what this is doing, if
we go over to this tape here--

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:46.520
everything's working
as expected-- we should

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:49.130
see this call come through.

00:45:49.130 --> 00:45:50.870
And there it is.

00:45:50.870 --> 00:45:53.360
So that's actually in progress.

00:45:53.360 --> 00:45:55.160
As is creating the
key, it's going out

00:45:55.160 --> 00:45:56.350
to the back end service.

00:45:56.350 --> 00:45:58.910
This lags a bit behind
on what's going on.

00:45:58.910 --> 00:46:03.470
And then you can see here
that it says, hey, here's

00:46:03.470 --> 00:46:04.280
your license key.

00:46:04.280 --> 00:46:07.100
And it's created my free trial.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:08.810
So let's go back real quick.

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:12.730
And we can take a look
at what's going on.

00:46:12.730 --> 00:46:16.170
So basically what Apigee allows
you to do is, in real time,

00:46:16.170 --> 00:46:18.070
we can take a look
at this request.

00:46:18.070 --> 00:46:22.230
This is where it verified our
API key that we use to send it.

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:24.630
Then there's some basic
authentication shapes in here

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:27.630
where we inject some
values into the request

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:30.960
before we send them off to
our actual back end service.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:34.410
And then, on the bottom
here, anything up top here,

00:46:34.410 --> 00:46:37.780
is the request pipeline before
it's called our service.

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:40.200
And then down here is
the response pipeline,

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:42.300
everything that gets
executed on the way out.

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:46.590
And there's error checking in
there, formatting, et cetera,

00:46:46.590 --> 00:46:50.370
makes it very easy to see what's
going on inside of your APIs

00:46:50.370 --> 00:46:51.390
as they're executing.

00:46:55.250 --> 00:47:00.590
And if we go here, this
is the XML to JSON shape.

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:07.030
And you can see here's
the JSON that's returned.

00:47:07.030 --> 00:47:11.400
If you go back one shape,
you can see here's the--

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:13.050
visible up there, sort of--

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:14.202
you can see here's the XML.

00:47:14.202 --> 00:47:16.410
It's kind of what they showed
in the keynote earlier.

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:19.230
This is actually the response
from the server in SOAP format,

00:47:19.230 --> 00:47:23.880
but we converted over to that
JSON format that we send back.

00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:26.920
Very useful.

00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:32.910
One of the last things I'll show
you is analytics and reporting.

00:47:32.910 --> 00:47:36.450
So one of the features that
we find extremely useful

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:41.010
is Apigee has a great
number of built-in reports.

00:47:44.650 --> 00:47:46.045
So if we go to--

00:48:04.450 --> 00:48:09.900
so by default, Apigee going
to give you proxy performance.

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:12.795
For example, it's going to give
you a bunch of default numbers.

00:48:12.795 --> 00:48:15.360
I've got to change
the date because this

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:17.140
is an older environment.

00:48:19.690 --> 00:48:20.190
Here we go.

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:23.180
So this will give you things
like average server response

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:26.630
time, amount of traffic
that came through the proxy,

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:27.860
et cetera, et cetera.

00:48:27.860 --> 00:48:29.980
And you could see
that, for example, you

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:32.840
know, for this period we
had 2.3 million calls.

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:34.100
This is all great.

00:48:34.100 --> 00:48:38.132
But every so often you need some
of your own custom analytics.

00:48:38.132 --> 00:48:39.590
We had a problem
a couple of months

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:43.880
ago where one user, for example,
was causing a lot of traffic

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:46.190
on our one proxy,
and we needed to know

00:48:46.190 --> 00:48:47.740
exactly which user that was.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:54.480
And those built in reports,
they give you high level

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:56.310
overall the total number.

00:48:56.310 --> 00:49:00.690
So what we needed is traffic by
the proxy and by the operation.

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:03.690
So we created this very
simple customer report.

00:49:03.690 --> 00:49:06.600
This took all about 15
minutes maybe to create.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:13.590
And you could see this gives us
here a quick drill down into,

00:49:13.590 --> 00:49:15.630
OK, over that time
period, here's

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:18.210
all the different
services that were called.

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:19.290
But we wanted more.

00:49:19.290 --> 00:49:22.020
We wanted to know exactly
which operations and who

00:49:22.020 --> 00:49:23.700
was calling at
because we had someone

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:26.290
that was causing a heavy load.

00:49:26.290 --> 00:49:28.960
So through this custom report,
it's very simple to take,

00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:34.270
for example, the contact
service, click on that.

00:49:34.270 --> 00:49:36.360
And this is going to
show me, OK, here's

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:37.710
all the different operations.

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:39.570
Here's the different paths.

00:49:39.570 --> 00:49:41.940
And then I can take the
Person Details, for example.

00:49:41.940 --> 00:49:43.140
I click on that.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:46.620
And now it's going
to tell me, OK.

00:49:46.620 --> 00:49:49.830
Here's the CWC Control
Center, and Control Center V2.

00:49:49.830 --> 00:49:52.650
That's where the majority
of your traffic is.

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:53.970
And this report also--

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:55.900
it's funny that
they're listed here--

00:49:55.900 --> 00:50:01.120
is actually, we-- they wanted
us to give them a new API key.

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:03.467
They said, we don't want
to use the old API anymore.

00:50:03.467 --> 00:50:04.550
Can you give us a new one?

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:06.310
We said, sure,
that's not a problem.

00:50:06.310 --> 00:50:08.020
They said, can you
turn off the old one?

00:50:08.020 --> 00:50:10.120
We said, we can, but
are you sure that none

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:11.980
of your other
applications are using it?

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:13.855
And they said, oh, yeah,
you can turn it off.

00:50:13.855 --> 00:50:15.160
Nobody's using it.

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:17.860
We ran this report,
and they were wrong.

00:50:17.860 --> 00:50:21.780
We said, no, no, if you look
right here, you could see V2,

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:24.640
you have 1,100 calls, for
example, or whatever it was.

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:26.380
And here you have 56,000.

00:50:26.380 --> 00:50:28.690
So this really helps
validate a lot of data,

00:50:28.690 --> 00:50:33.350
examine your traffic,
and see what's going on.

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:35.568
So lastly, can we switch
back to the slides?

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:39.854
Cool.

00:50:39.854 --> 00:50:41.520
So overall, I just
wanted to talk to you

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:43.410
in general about a
lot of the benefits

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:47.100
that we at Citrix find
from the Apigee solution.

00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:50.699
Consistent APIs is a facade,
you know, disparate backgrounds.

00:50:50.699 --> 00:50:52.740
You know, I assured you
guys, we have a whole lot

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:55.620
of SOAP services, and then
we have a lot of new services

00:50:55.620 --> 00:50:58.350
that are web API
services, et cetera.

00:50:58.350 --> 00:51:02.100
So Apigee allows us to
use them to sit on top,

00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:03.720
call out to these
different services,

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:06.870
and expose them over whatever
protocols that we want.

00:51:06.870 --> 00:51:09.300
In fact, one of our services,
the single sign on service

00:51:09.300 --> 00:51:11.880
that we have that
gives out tokens,

00:51:11.880 --> 00:51:13.680
several third
parties and vendors

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:18.180
use this to authenticate
against the Citrix users.

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:20.190
And they said,
well, is there a way

00:51:20.190 --> 00:51:23.387
we can also get like, someone's
first name, last name,

00:51:23.387 --> 00:51:25.470
and about five or six other
pieces of information?

00:51:25.470 --> 00:51:27.740
So we have a second
single sign on API

00:51:27.740 --> 00:51:29.820
that actually authenticates,
and at the same time

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:33.090
calls another service, but
combines all that data into one

00:51:33.090 --> 00:51:33.900
response.

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:35.820
We didn't have to write
additional services

00:51:35.820 --> 00:51:36.550
on the back end.

00:51:36.550 --> 00:51:38.216
All we needed to do
was configure Apigee

00:51:38.216 --> 00:51:42.120
and say, hey, make a call
out to the second service.

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:45.480
And Apigee, they allow us to
have our developers focusing

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:48.090
on actually writing
these back end services,

00:51:48.090 --> 00:51:50.850
as opposed to, OK, now I have
to figure out a new security

00:51:50.850 --> 00:51:53.250
implementation, like
Greg was talking about.

00:51:53.250 --> 00:51:55.330
Or if I wanted to consume
that schema I showed

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:56.880
you guys a couple
of slides ago, I

00:51:56.880 --> 00:51:59.910
wouldn't want to be the
guy that had to do that.

00:51:59.910 --> 00:52:04.440
Apigee also, they took away
our need to manage these users.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:06.360
We manage them now
through the Apigee system.

00:52:06.360 --> 00:52:08.443
Before, we had to create
Active Directory accounts

00:52:08.443 --> 00:52:09.920
in some situations.

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:12.080
In others, there was
databases of passwords.

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:14.270
And canceling one was
a whole other hassle.

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:15.390
Apigee is like, we got it.

00:52:15.390 --> 00:52:16.640
We'll take care of it for you.

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:19.370
Makes it easy for us,
and much, much easier

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:22.790
for developers and consumers
because it's easier for them

00:52:22.790 --> 00:52:26.810
to get on board working in
a couple of minutes almost.

00:52:26.810 --> 00:52:30.645
Lastly, they can secure
and obfuscate data for us.

00:52:30.645 --> 00:52:32.270
You guys didn't see
it in this service,

00:52:32.270 --> 00:52:36.620
but we have a vendor ID that we
need in our back end service.

00:52:36.620 --> 00:52:38.810
And it would be really
silly for me to say to you

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:41.930
as an end user, hey, your
idea is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:52:41.930 --> 00:52:44.640
please send me that
in every request.

00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:47.390
So what we can actually do is
be associated with their API

00:52:47.390 --> 00:52:48.920
key inside of Apigee.

00:52:48.920 --> 00:52:52.029
When Prithpal was shown
you guys the screen

00:52:52.029 --> 00:52:53.570
where he was approving
the developer,

00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:55.700
there were some key value
pairs at the bottom.

00:52:55.700 --> 00:52:58.280
So we set that up in there so
the end user actually doesn't

00:52:58.280 --> 00:52:59.480
need to know that value.

00:52:59.480 --> 00:53:04.010
We pull it from there develop
project inside Apigee.

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:07.220
And finally, I showed you guys
it has traffic management where

00:53:07.220 --> 00:53:09.500
we can see what's going
on the different servers,

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:11.690
and we can run
reports, analytics,

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:15.020
and see everything that's
going on from the Apigee side

00:53:15.020 --> 00:53:16.960
with our APIs.

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:21.610
So on that note, I'd like
to bring Greg back up.

00:53:21.610 --> 00:53:23.570
Talk about a couple
of last minute things.

00:53:23.570 --> 00:53:26.195
GREG BRAIL: Yeah, I think I have
one or two last minute points.

00:53:26.195 --> 00:53:27.970
We're almost out of time.

00:53:27.970 --> 00:53:29.980
First of all, as we
saw, I mean, Adam

00:53:29.980 --> 00:53:32.860
did a great job of showing
real life production traffic,

00:53:32.860 --> 00:53:34.420
which I thought was awesome.

00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:36.580
There's no fakery there at all.

00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:37.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:40.300
And so, I really
appreciated that.

00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:42.310
But I mean, really,
consistent message here.

00:53:42.310 --> 00:53:43.916
Think about developers.

00:53:43.916 --> 00:53:45.790
Put yourself in the
shoes of your developers.

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:49.540
And build APIs that you think
they're going to want to do.

00:53:49.540 --> 00:53:51.610
Many people, sometimes
they question

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:53.050
the value of self-service.

00:53:53.050 --> 00:53:56.050
But we just saw an example of
where, by enabling self-service

00:53:56.050 --> 00:53:58.360
for developers,
Citrix is able to make

00:53:58.360 --> 00:54:00.690
things a lot more
efficient internally.

00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:02.382
So thank you all for coming.

00:54:02.382 --> 00:54:04.090
Thanks for staying
around the conference.

00:54:04.090 --> 00:54:06.080
And we'll see you all later.

00:54:06.080 --> 00:54:08.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

